WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.620
What do fork, exit and

2
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.260
wait actually do? Fork creates

3
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.340
a child process, exit ends

4
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.460
execution, and wait allows a

5
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.040
parent to collect a child

6
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.160
result. The trick is to

7
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.640
follow two execution histories instead

8
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.020
of pretending that one line

9
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.760
of code still means only

10
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.520
one process.

11
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.940
Imagine a parent with process

12
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.240
identifier ten reaching a successful

13
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.560
fork. The operating system creates

14
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.820
a child, which we label

15
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.400
eleven in this authored example.

16
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.420
Parent and child then have

17
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.960
distinct process identities, even though

18
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.680
they continue from corresponding points

19
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.360
in the program.

20
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.340
The successful return value distinguishes

21
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.360
the two paths. The parent

22
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.120
receives the child identifier, eleven

23
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.040
here, while the child receives

24
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.300
zero. Failure is a separate

25
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.300
case, so do not invent

26
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.040
a successful child when fork

27
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.020
reports an error.

28
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.520
Both processes can now make

29
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.740
progress under the scheduler. The

30
00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.320
child may run first or

31
00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.740
the parent may run first

32
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.320
unless some explicit synchronization orders

33
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.460
them. A neat parent-before-child printout

34
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.740
from one run is an

35
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.260
observation, not a scheduling guarantee.

36
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.100
Their ordinary private memory is

37
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.500
logically separate. If each changes

38
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.460
its own private counter, that

39
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.040
is not a shared-memory race

40
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.380
on a single counter. File-descriptor

41
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.260
references can still refer to

42
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.700
shared underlying kernel objects, which

43
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.340
is why resource ownership needs

44
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.440
a more careful diagram.

45
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.160
Suppose the child finishes and

46
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.760
calls exit before the parent

47
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.240
calls wait. In the xv6

48
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.180
lab, the child becomes a

49
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.820
zombie: it is no longer

50
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.720
executing, but enough process-table information

51
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.040
remains for the parent to

52
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.180
collect its status. Zombie does

53
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.620
not mean a process secretly

54
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.120
keeps using the processor.

55
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.660
When the parent waits and

56
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.140
the child has exited, the

57
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.880
kernel can return that child

58
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.980
result and reclaim its remaining

59
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.940
process-table slot. If no suitable

60
00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.940
child has finished yet, wait

61
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.560
can block the parent until

62
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.120
the required event. Waiting changes

63
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.780
which process is ready to

64
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:06.300
execute.

65
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.520
The lab makes this visible

66
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.260
by delaying the parent, then

67
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.700
using the process dump to

68
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.660
observe a zombie. After reaping,

69
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.700
the retained entry disappears or

70
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.920
becomes reusable. In that lab

71
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.280
branch, sleep arguments are clock

72
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.980
ticks, so do not read

73
00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.820
an arbitrary sleep value as

74
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:24.200
seconds.

75
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.200
A parent can also wait

76
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.300
before the child completes, producing

77
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.980
the opposite order of events.

78
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.300
The dependency is still that

79
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.200
wait collects an exited child,

80
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.720
not that the child must

81
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.320
run immediately after fork. Trace

82
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.160
the actual return, block, exit

83
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.480
and wakeup events.

84
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.500
Things to take note of.

85
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.040
Separate process identity, fork return

86
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.360
values, private memory and shared

87
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.740
kernel references. Explain the zombie

88
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.240
interval and the later reaping

89
00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.400
event; neither an exit nor

90
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.160
a sampled print order proves

91
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.540
that every piece of process

92
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.160
bookkeeping has already vanished.
